\title{ecem: Elicited Priors for Coarsening Regimes in Coarsened Exact Matching}
\description{
Coarsened exact matching (CEM) requires the analyst to fix a coarsening
(a set of cutpoints) for every covariate before matching. \strong{ecem}
replaces that single fixed choice with an elicited \emph{prior} over
cutpoints, draws repeatedly from it, matches and estimates on each draw,
and pools the results -- along with diagnostics for when that pooling is
(and is not) trustworthy.
}
\section{Specifying a covariate's coarsening}{

Every covariate in a \code{cutpoint_specs} list is one of five things -- see
\code{\link[=regime]{regime()}} and \code{\link[=draw_cutpoints_for_var]{draw_cutpoints_for_var()}} for the full description:
a list of ranges (elicited, \code{X_E}), a plain numeric vector (fixed,
\code{X_F}), the string \code{"exact"}, \code{NULL} (excluded), or a \code{\link[=regime]{regime()}} object
(competing elicited cutpoint sets for one covariate).
}

\section{The core workflow}{

\code{\link[=run_M_draws]{run_M_draws()}} draws (or, when the achievable-configuration count is
small enough, exactly enumerates -- see \code{exact_if_K_leq}) repeated
coarsenings, matches, and estimates on each; \code{\link[=pool_draws]{pool_draws()}} combines
them via Rubin's rules. \code{\link[=pooling_diagnostics]{pooling_diagnostics()}} bundles the checks for
whether that pooling is valid (\code{\link[=flatness_test_XE]{flatness_test_XE()}},
\code{\link[=excess_variance_test]{excess_variance_test()}}, and the retention-interaction diagnostic) into
one call; if pooling is not valid, \code{\link[=existence_test]{existence_test()}} provides a
fallback (and can reuse \code{\link[=pooling_diagnostics]{pooling_diagnostics()}}'s bootstrap rather than
rerunning one). \code{\link[=label_diagnostics]{label_diagnostics()}} bundles \code{\link[=cov_att_ate]{cov_att_ate()}} and
\code{\link[=cov_retention_per_draw]{cov_retention_per_draw()}}, the covariance-based diagnostics for
labeling the pooled estimate as an FSATT, ATT, or ATE.
