\section{The within-draw estimator}{

\code{\link[=fit_effect]{fit_effect()}} estimates each draw's effect on its own matched sample
via a CEM-weighted regression by default (\code{estimator = "regression"},
\code{\link[=run_M_draws]{run_M_draws()}}'s and \code{\link[=run_draw]{run_draw()}}'s default too) -- the "run whatever
model you would have run anyway, on the matched data" approach the CEM
literature itself recommends -- or, via \code{estimator = "mean_diff"}, a
simpler treated-count-weighted stratum mean-difference with no
regression adjustment. Either way, the covariate-indexed \code{tau(x)}
surface the covariance diagnostics need is always the stratum
mean-difference version, regardless of which estimator produces the
headline \code{tau_hat}.
